# Codex Customization Reference

This is a practical reference for customizing Codex for personal work, one repository, or a shared team workflow. It covers instructions, skills, custom agents, MCP servers, plugins, hooks, and configuration.

## 1. The mental model

Codex customization is a set of complementary layers. Do not force every need into one file.

| Need | Use | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One temporary instruction | The current prompt/task | It should disappear after the task. |
| Rules that apply whenever Codex works in a repository | `AGENTS.md` | Durable repository guidance: commands, conventions, review expectations. |
| A reusable workflow | A skill | Supports an on-demand workflow with instructions, references, scripts, and assets. |
| A specialized parallel worker | A custom agent / subagent | Gives a spawned agent a defined role, model, tool surface, and instructions. |
| Live access to an external system | MCP | Connects Codex to tools and context outside the repository. |
| A distributable package of skills, MCP/app wiring, hooks, and assets | A plugin | The installable/sharable unit. |
| Deterministic enforcement at a lifecycle point | A hook plus normal project tooling | Use for mechanical checks, not general advice. |
| Default model, sandbox, MCP, hook, or agent settings | `config.toml` | Configuration rather than workflow guidance. |
| Helpful learned context from prior work | Memories | Personal context; do not use it as a source-controlled policy. |

**Rule of thumb:** `AGENTS.md` shapes normal behavior, skills package repeatable procedures, custom agents split specialized work, MCP connects outside systems, and plugins distribute a reusable bundle.

## 2. Scope and precedence

There are three useful scopes.

| Scope | Use it for | Typical location |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Personal | Your preferences and reusable workflows across repositories | `~/.codex/`, `~/.agents/` |
| Project | Codebase and team-specific behavior; commit it to Git | Repository root and its subdirectories |
| Plugin / team distribution | Reuse across many projects or teammates | A plugin repository plus a marketplace entry |

`~` means the home directory of the environment running Codex.

- In Windows Codex, it is normally your Windows user home, for example `C:\Users\Khaled Ragab`.
- In VS Code Remote - WSL, it is the Linux user home inside that WSL distribution, for example `/home/khaled`.
- A Windows Codex profile and a WSL Codex profile can therefore have different global files. For shared project behavior, keep files in the repository.

### `AGENTS.md` precedence

Codex builds an instruction chain at the start of a run:

1. In Codex Home, it reads `AGENTS.override.md` when present; otherwise it reads `AGENTS.md`.
2. Starting at the repository root, it walks down to the current working directory.
3. In each directory, it prefers `AGENTS.override.md`, then `AGENTS.md`.
4. Files closer to the current working directory appear later and therefore override broader guidance.

Keep `AGENTS.md` files short. The default combined instruction limit is 32 KiB.

## 3. Personal developer setup

Use this when you want consistent personal defaults across all repositories.

```text
~/.codex/
  AGENTS.md                         # Personal coding preferences
  AGENTS.override.md                # Optional temporary global override
  config.toml                       # Global configuration: MCP, model, sandbox, hooks, agents
  agents/
    reviewer.toml                   # Personal custom agent
    explorer.toml                   # Personal custom agent
  plugins/
    my-team-tools/                  # Installed or locally developed plugin

~/.agents/
  skills/
    human-crafted-frontend-design/
      SKILL.md                       # Personal skill, available across repositories
  plugins/
    marketplace.json                 # Personal plugin marketplace catalog
```

Use personal scope for preferences such as response style, your preferred package manager, a generic release-check workflow, or a personal code-review agent.

Do not store team-only rules, private project schemas, or repository-specific commands in your personal global files.

### Minimal global `AGENTS.md`

```md
# Working Agreements

- Prefer the package manager already used by the repository.
- Run the relevant tests before declaring a code change complete.
- Do not add production dependencies without explaining why.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, or `.env` files.
```

## 4. Recommended project structure

Start with only the folders you actually need.

```text
my-app/
  AGENTS.md                         # Repository rules and commands
  .agents/
    skills/
      human-crafted-frontend-design/
        SKILL.md                     # Project-specific skill
    plugins/
      marketplace.json               # Only when the repo exposes local plugins
  .codex/
    config.toml                      # Trusted project settings, if needed
    agents/
      reviewer.toml                  # Project-specific custom agent
      pr-explorer.toml               # Project-specific custom agent
  plugins/
    project-workflows/               # Only when authoring a local plugin
      .codex-plugin/
        plugin.json
      skills/
        release-check/
          SKILL.md
  src/
  tests/
  package.json
```

For a small application, this is often enough:

```text
my-app/
  AGENTS.md
  .agents/
    skills/
      human-crafted-frontend-design/
        SKILL.md
  src/
  tests/
```

Add `.codex/config.toml`, custom agents, MCP, or plugins only when the problem calls for them.

## 5. `AGENTS.md`: durable repository guidance

`AGENTS.md` is plain Markdown. It is read before work begins; it is not a selectable skill and it needs no YAML frontmatter.

### Put these things in `AGENTS.md`

- Install, build, lint, test, and local development commands.
- Project architecture and directory conventions.
- Code review expectations.
- Security boundaries and generated-file restrictions.
- Repeated corrections you give Codex.
- Routing hints, such as which directory or document to inspect first.

### Do not put these things in `AGENTS.md`

- A one-off task request.
- Long API schemas or large reference documents.
- Secrets.
- A large optional workflow that only applies sometimes. Make that a skill instead.

### Project-level example

```md
# Repository Guidance

## Commands

- Install: `pnpm install`
- Develop: `pnpm dev`
- Lint: `pnpm lint`
- Test: `pnpm test`
- Build: `pnpm build`

## Working Rules

- Reuse existing components and utilities before introducing a dependency.
- Keep API validation in `src/lib/validation`.
- Do not expose server environment variables to client code.
- Add or update tests when shared behavior changes.

## Boundaries

- Do not edit `src/generated` manually.
- Do not commit `.env` files or credentials.
```

### Nested specialization

Use a nested file when rules change for one part of a monorepo.

```text
my-app/
  AGENTS.md
  services/
    payments/
      AGENTS.override.md
```

The nested override has priority when Codex works from `services/payments/`. It replaces an `AGENTS.md` in that same directory, while still layering over root and global guidance.

## 6. Skills: reusable workflows

A skill is a folder that gives Codex a reusable procedure, domain expertise, helper scripts, references, or output assets.

Use a skill for work such as:

- Building a frontend using your design standards.
- Preparing a release.
- Reviewing API changes.
- Updating documentation from a defined source.
- Generating a project-specific report.

### Skill scope and locations

```text
# Personal skill
~/.agents/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md

# Repository skill
<repo>/.agents/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md
```

Important: current Codex documentation places **personal user skills** in `~/.agents/skills`, while `~/.codex` is used for Codex Home configuration, global guidance, custom agents, and plugins. Do not put custom work inside a managed `.system` skill folder.

### Skill file structure

```text
human-crafted-frontend-design/
  SKILL.md                           # Required
  agents/
    openai.yaml                      # Optional UI metadata
  scripts/
    check-ui.ps1                     # Optional deterministic helper
  references/
    product-rules.md                 # Optional detailed material
  assets/
    starter-template/                # Optional output resources
```

Each skill gets its own folder and one required file named exactly `SKILL.md`. Do not combine unrelated skills into one giant Markdown file.

### Required `SKILL.md` format

```md
---
name: skill-name
description: Explain exactly what the skill does and the tasks that should trigger it.
---

# Workflow

1. Inspect the relevant project guidance.
2. Follow the defined procedure.
3. Run the required verification.
4. Report the result and remaining risks.
```

Rules for the frontmatter:

- `name` is lowercase, uses letters, digits, and hyphens only, and matches the folder name.
- `description` is the trigger mechanism. Put all "when to use" information there.
- Use only `name` and `description` in the required frontmatter.

### How skills control context

Skills use progressive disclosure:

1. Codex sees each skill's `name` and `description` for discovery.
2. Codex loads the `SKILL.md` body only after selecting the skill.
3. Codex reads references or runs scripts only when needed.

Keep the main `SKILL.md` concise. Put large schemas, API documents, and long examples in `references/`; put repeatable mechanical work in tested scripts.

### Explicit versus automatic use

- Invoke a skill explicitly from the skills UI or slash menu when available.
- Ask for it by name, for example: "Use `human-crafted-frontend-design` to redesign this page."
- Codex can also choose a skill implicitly when the task matches its `description`.

If a skill is not detected, check the folder location, exact filename `SKILL.md`, YAML frontmatter, current host (Windows versus WSL), and restart or start a new task.

### Converting a Markdown specification into a skill

An ordinary Markdown document is not auto-discovered as a skill. Add the frontmatter, place it in the correct folder, and keep the existing content as the body. For example:

```md
---
name: human-crafted-frontend-design
description: Create or improve premium, calm, readable, product-first web interfaces. Use for new frontend screens, app shells, dashboards, design systems, responsive layouts, and UI redesigns.
---

# Purpose

Generate frontend UI directions and implementation guidance for modern web apps.

# Required Output

- UI direction.
- Layout structure.
- Component recommendations.
- Typography and spacing systems.
- Color and motion rules.
- Empty, loading, and error state guidance.
```

Use `$skill-creator` when you want Codex to scaffold a new skill, generate optional UI metadata, and validate its structure. Use Record & Replay when the workflow is easier to demonstrate than describe.

## 7. Custom agents and subagents

There are two related concepts:

- A **subagent workflow**: Codex spawns focused agents in parallel and collects summaries into the main task.
- A **custom agent**: a reusable configuration for a kind of subagent, with a name, role, instructions, and optional model, reasoning, sandbox, skills, and MCP settings.

Use subagents for independent, mostly read-heavy work: exploration, test analysis, log triage, documentation verification, or parallel code review. Be careful with parallel writes because concurrent edits increase conflicts and coordination cost.

Subagents reduce noise in the main task by returning summaries, but they do **not** reduce total model cost automatically. Each subagent does its own tool and model work.

### Custom agent locations

```text
# Personal custom agents
~/.codex/agents/<agent-name>.toml

# Repository custom agents
<repo>/.codex/agents/<agent-name>.toml
```

Each TOML file defines one agent. Required fields are `name`, `description`, and `developer_instructions`.

### Minimal custom agent

```toml
name = "reviewer"
description = "PR reviewer focused on correctness, security, behavior regressions, and missing tests."
developer_instructions = """
Review code like an owner.
Prioritize correctness, security, behavior regressions, and missing test coverage.
Lead with concrete findings and avoid style-only comments unless they hide a real bug.
"""
```

### Read-only project explorer

```toml
name = "pr-explorer"
description = "Read-only codebase explorer that gathers evidence before changes are proposed."
model = "gpt-5.6-terra"
model_reasoning_effort = "medium"
sandbox_mode = "read-only"
developer_instructions = """
Stay in exploration mode.
Trace real execution paths, cite files and symbols, and do not make changes.
Prefer targeted search and file reads over broad scans.
"""
```

Optional custom-agent fields include `nickname_candidates`, `model`, `model_reasoning_effort`, `sandbox_mode`, `mcp_servers`, and `skills.config`. Omitted settings inherit from the parent task.

### Requesting agents in a task

```text
Review this branch against main. Spawn three agents in parallel:
1. An explorer to map affected code paths.
2. A reviewer to find bugs, security issues, and missing tests.
3. A docs researcher to verify framework APIs.
Wait for all three and return a concise summary with file references.
```

Project guidance and skills can also tell Codex when to delegate, but use that sparingly. Good custom agents are narrow and opinionated.

## 8. MCP: external tools and context

MCP stands for Model Context Protocol. It connects Codex to external tools and data providers, such as Figma, GitHub, Linear, browser tooling, documentation, Sentry, internal services, or a company knowledge system.

An MCP server can expose:

- **Tools**: actions, such as creating an issue or querying a service.
- **Resources**: readable external data.
- **Prompts**: reusable prompt templates.

Use MCP when the required capability or information exists outside the local repository. Do not create an MCP server just to store static instructions; use a skill or reference file for that.

### MCP scope

```text
# Global MCP configuration
~/.codex/config.toml

# Project MCP configuration; use only for a trusted project
<repo>/.codex/config.toml
```

The ChatGPT desktop app, Codex CLI, and IDE extension share MCP configuration when they use the same Codex host. A VS Code Remote - WSL session uses the WSL host, which can have a different `~/.codex/config.toml` from Windows.

### MCP configuration examples

STDIO server:

```toml
[mcp_servers.context7]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp"]
env_vars = ["LOCAL_TOKEN"]
```

Remote HTTP server:

```toml
[mcp_servers.figma]
url = "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp"
bearer_token_env_var = "FIGMA_OAUTH_TOKEN"
enabled_tools = ["get_file", "get_nodes"]
default_tools_approval_mode = "prompt"
```

Never paste API tokens directly into a repository `config.toml`. Reference environment variable names, use OAuth when available, and only enable the tools the workflow actually needs.

### Best pairing: skill + MCP

Use a skill to define the procedure and MCP to provide the live capability.

```text
Skill: "Turn approved Figma designs into code"
  -> inspect the selected design
  -> use the Figma MCP tools
  -> follow repository design rules
  -> implement and verify the page
```

## 9. Plugins: installable distribution packages

A plugin is for distribution, not the first thing to build. Start with a local skill while iterating. Build a plugin when you need to share a stable workflow across teams, bundle skills with connectors/MCP configuration, package hooks, or publish a reusable package.

A plugin can contain skills, an MCP-backed app, or both.

### Minimal plugin structure

```text
my-first-plugin/
  .codex-plugin/
    plugin.json
  skills/
    hello/
      SKILL.md
```

`my-first-plugin/.codex-plugin/plugin.json`:

```json
{
  "name": "my-first-plugin",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Reusable workflow package",
  "skills": "./skills/"
}
```

Use a stable kebab-case plugin name. The plugin manifest is required.

### Personal and repository plugin marketplaces

```text
# Repository marketplace and local plugin storage
<repo>/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json
<repo>/plugins/<plugin-name>/

# Personal marketplace and local plugin storage
~/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json
~/.codex/plugins/<plugin-name>/
```

Use `$plugin-creator` to scaffold the plugin, its `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, and a personal marketplace entry. Use a plugin only after the skill/workflow has proven itself in real tasks.

## 10. `config.toml`: settings, not instructions

Use `config.toml` for Codex settings such as model defaults, sandbox behavior, MCP servers, hooks, and agent limits. Do not use it as a replacement for `AGENTS.md` or a skill.

```text
# Personal
~/.codex/config.toml

# Trusted project
<repo>/.codex/config.toml
```

Example agent limits:

```toml
[agents]
max_threads = 4
max_depth = 1
```

`max_depth = 1` lets the main task spawn direct child agents without allowing unlimited recursive delegation. Keep it low unless you have a clear reason to increase it.

## 11. Hooks and ordinary enforcement tools

Use a hook for an action that must happen reliably at a lifecycle point around tool calls, commands, or edits. Examples include recording an audit event or blocking a forbidden command.

Prefer normal engineering enforcement for normal engineering rules:

- Formatter for formatting.
- Linter for style and static checks.
- Type checker for type safety.
- Tests for behavior.
- Pre-commit or CI checks for enforcement before merge.

Put the human explanation in `AGENTS.md`; let automated tools enforce it mechanically.

## 12. Apps, connectors, and browser/computer tools

Apps and connectors give Codex authorized access to third-party workspaces, such as GitHub, Figma, Google Drive, Notion, Slack, Linear, or internal tools. They may be bundled in plugins or exposed through MCP.

- Use an app/connector when the data is private and authorized access is needed.
- Use MCP when you need local or custom tool integration.
- Use browser/computer tools for interaction and verification when there is no more direct API or connector.
- Check workspace or administrator policy: availability and authorization can vary by account and surface.

## 13. WSL and Windows guidance

If the code lives in WSL and VS Code uses Remote - WSL:

- Treat WSL as the working host for the extension.
- Put project guidance and project skills in the repository so they travel with the code.
- Put WSL-only personal files under WSL `~/.codex` and `~/.agents`.
- Put Windows-only personal files under Windows `%USERPROFILE%\.codex` and `%USERPROFILE%\.agents`.
- Do not assume a global file created on Windows will automatically be visible to a Codex process running inside WSL.

For cross-machine and cross-surface consistency, version-control the repository files and publish reusable team workflows as a plugin.

## 14. A sensible rollout workflow

1. Add a small repository `AGENTS.md` with commands, conventions, and safety boundaries.
2. Work normally for a few tasks.
3. When you repeat a multi-step process, create a project skill under `.agents/skills`.
4. Move long reference material to `references/` and repeatable mechanics to tested `scripts/`.
5. Add MCP only when the skill needs live data or external actions.
6. Create a custom agent only for a genuinely distinct role, such as a read-only explorer, reviewer, or documentation researcher.
7. Use subagents for independent investigation, testing, and review; avoid parallel writes unless work is isolated.
8. Turn a proven skill/MCP/hook bundle into a plugin when it must be shared across projects or people.
9. Keep tokens under control: keep `AGENTS.md` small, skill descriptions precise, references on-demand, and subagents limited to work that benefits from parallelism.

## 15. Troubleshooting checklist

### A skill does not appear or trigger

- Confirm the folder is in `~/.agents/skills/<name>/` or `<repo>/.agents/skills/<name>/`.
- Confirm the file is exactly `SKILL.md`.
- Confirm YAML frontmatter begins and ends with `---` and has `name` plus `description`.
- Confirm the name is lowercase kebab-case and matches the folder.
- Start a new task or restart the relevant Codex surface.
- If using WSL, verify the file is in the WSL filesystem, not only on Windows.
- Make the description more explicit about triggering tasks.

### Guidance seems wrong or stale

- Look for `AGENTS.override.md` in Codex Home or a nearer directory.
- Ask Codex to summarize active instructions from the current directory.
- Check that the file is non-empty.
- Restart the session after changing global guidance or configuration.

### An MCP server is unavailable

- Check that it is enabled in Settings or `config.toml`.
- Check authentication or run its OAuth login flow.
- Verify the server command, URL, environment variable name, and working directory.
- Restrict dangerous tools with allow lists and approval modes.
- Restart the app or extension after changing configuration.

### A plugin does not appear

- Confirm `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` exists and is valid.
- Confirm the marketplace entry points to the intended plugin directory.
- Restart the ChatGPT desktop app after changing a local marketplace or plugin.
- Check workspace policy and authentication requirements.

